---
title: Appreciating Land, Declining Use
authors: [Sara Shah]
broadcast: 4
order: 1
start: "2:53"
---

*A Vignette about Chinatown’s Real Estate Market*

Manhattan’s Chinatown presents a strange real-estate contradiction: the neighborhood is increasingly valuable as property while becoming increasingly difficult to sustain as a neighborhood. Over the last decade, rents in Chinatown increased by roughly 5 percent while household incomes grew by only 2 percent. The problem, then, is relative affordability: even modest increases in housing and operating costs become destabilizing when the incomes of the people and businesses paying them remain nearly stagnant. Meanwhile, today, more than a quarter of renter households in the broader Lower East Side - Chinatown area are severely rent burdened, spending more than half of their income on rent.{% ref n=1 /%} At the same time, Chinatown occupies increasingly valuable Lower Manhattan land; a 2022 New York State study found land sales in the area averaging approximately $1,600 per square foot.{% ref n=2 /%}

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/sara-4.jpg" alt="Bar chart comparing rent growth and income growth over the last decade in Chinatown and citywide" pie="Over the last decade: Chinatown rent +5% vs. citywide +25%. Chinatown income +2% vs. citywide +19%. The gap that matters is affordability, not price" ancho="completo" /%}

This produces a growing separation between the value of property and the viability of its use. A Chinatown building is valued not only by what its existing apartments, restaurants, or shops can afford to pay, but by its Lower Manhattan location and expectations of what the property could generate in the future. In this market, vacancy can itself have value. Rather than accepting a substantially lower rent, an owner may wait for a higher-paying tenant, reposition the property, sell it, or preserve the possibility of redevelopment. Research on New York storefronts describes this as an “option value” of vacancy: under certain market conditions, waiting can be economically preferable to immediately leasing a space.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/sara-5.jpg" alt="A shuttered corner storefront in Chinatown with Space for Rent and Corner Store for Lease signs" /%}

88 East Broadway makes this contradiction physical. Once home to more than 100 small businesses and a major commercial and social hub for Chinatown’s Fujianese immigrant community, the East Broadway Mall had declined to just 23 remaining vendors by 2023.{% ref n=4 /%} Its deterioration was not simply a disappearance of demand. The mall’s operating structure became increasingly difficult to sustain: its annual rent rose over time, while the payment it made in lieu of property taxes increased from $43,000 annually to more than $700,000 according to the city’s assessment.{% ref n=5 /%} The operator ultimately filed for bankruptcy in 2019, while disputes over rents, leases, management, and the property’s future left longtime merchants facing continued uncertainty.{% ref n=6 /%} The result is a building whose strategic value as Lower Manhattan real estate persists while it functions at only a fraction of its former neighborhood capacity.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/sara-6.jpg" alt="A man sitting on a stool outside a jewelry stall in the East Broadway Mall, beside rows of closed metal shutters" /%}

Yet directly across the street, a different commercial economy is emerging. At 75 East Broadway, boutique fashion brands such as Eckhaus Latta have moved into the same Chinatown mall typology from which longstanding immigrant businesses are disappearing. For these newer retailers, Chinatown offers both cultural identity and strategic location - distinctive, unconventional spaces just blocks from SoHo’s established fashion and retail economy. The contrast suggests that these spaces are not simply becoming commercially obsolete; rather, the economy capable of occupying them is changing. As cultural and community-serving businesses struggle to remain, the same spaces become desirable to a new generation of fashion, art, and design retailers whose customers and economic networks extend well beyond Chinatown.

{% galeria %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/sara-7.jpg" alt="The storefronts of the 2 Bridge Mall at 75 East Broadway under the Manhattan Bridge approach" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/sara-8.jpg" alt="A rack of clothes in the Eckhaus Latta store, with the East Broadway Mall sign visible through the window" /%}
{% /galeria %}

Ting’s Gift Shop makes this separation visible at the scale of a single storefront. Opened in 1958 at the corner of Doyers and Pell Streets, the family-run shop operated for 66 years before closing in 2024.{% ref n=3 /%} The nearby Wing on Wo & Co. confronted the property question even more directly. Before fifth-generation owner Mei Lum took over the business, her family was considering selling the building - a decision she ultimately understood as part of a larger pattern of property turnover, business closure, and displacement in Chinatown.{% ref n=5 /%}

{% galeria %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/sara-9.jpg" alt="Two women in matching white T-shirts in front of the red door of Ting’s Gift Shop at 18 Doyers Street" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/sara-10.jpg" alt="Mei Lum in front of the Wing On Wo & Co. storefront at 26 Mott Street" /%}
{% /galeria %}

New development does not necessarily resolve this imbalance. Between 2010 and 2025, 5,994 units were constructed in buildings across the Lower East Side - Chinatown area; approximately 66 percent were market-rate.{% ref n=7 /%} The result is not straightforward gentrification. Chinatown can become more valuable as property while becoming less viable as a cultural and economic enclave. The neighborhood is therefore gaining real-estate value and housing without necessarily gaining housing that its existing population can afford.The land can succeed economically while the neighborhood it supports struggles to survive.

{% fuentes %}
1. NYU Furman Center, “Lower East Side/Chinatown,” NYC Neighborhood Data Profiles, updated May 6, 2026. The Furman Center reports that 25.2 percent of renter households in Lower East Side/Chinatown were severely rent burdened in 2024 and that growth in median gross rent had outpaced growth in median household income by 17.4 percentage points.
2. New York State, Chinatown Downtown Revitalization Initiative: Strategic Investment Plan (2022), I-54. The report notes average land sales of approximately $1,600 per square foot and connects rising land values partly to demand for market-rate development.
4. April Xu and Amir Khafagy, “Behind a Chinatown Real Estate Deal, a Web of Shifting Alliances and Political Connections,” Documented, October 23, 2023. The East Broadway Mall once contained more than 100 local businesses; by 2023, only 23 vendors remained under lease.
5. Xu and Khafagy, “Behind a Chinatown Real Estate Deal.” The original lease required annual rent of $432,000, increasing 20 percent every ten years, in addition to a payment in lieu of taxes (PILOT). According to DCAS, the PILOT assessment had reached $703,897.56 annually by 2023.
6. Xu and Khafagy, “Behind a Chinatown Real Estate Deal.” East Broadway Mall Inc. filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy in July 2019. In 2023, the City approved the process of transferring the mall’s lease to a successor operator, Broadway East Group LLC.
7. NYU Furman Center, “Lower East Side/Chinatown.” Between 2010 and 2025, 5,994 units were constructed in buildings containing four or more units; 3,957, or approximately 66 percent, were market-rate.
{% /fuentes %}
